---
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Photo from '../components/Photo.astro';
import AudioPlayer from '../components/AudioPlayer.astro';
import BellLab from '../components/physics/BellLab.astro';
import ConesDiagram from '../components/physics/ConesDiagram.astro';
import ClassicalProof from '../components/physics/ClassicalProof.astro';
import { formatDateShort } from '../lib/format';

import pumpLaser from '../assets/photos/2024-bruqner-making-of/pump-laser-crystal.jpg';
import setupBeams from '../assets/photos/setup/setup-with-beams.jpg';
import caseTop from '../assets/photos/setup/case-top-view.jpg';

const measured = (await getCollection('shows', (s) => s.data.bell !== undefined && !s.data.draft))
  .sort((a, b) => +a.data.date - +b.data.date)
  .map((s) => ({ label: `${s.data.city}, ${formatDateShort(s.data.date)}`, value: s.data.bell!, id: s.id }));

const sections = [
  { id: 'chance', title: 'Chance' },
  { id: 'entanglement', title: 'Entanglement' },
  { id: 'bell', title: "Bell's test" },
  { id: 'try', title: 'Run it yourself' },
  { id: 'pairs', title: 'Making the pairs' },
  { id: 'measuring', title: 'Measuring' },
  { id: 'limits', title: 'What it does not show' },
];

const lineage = [
  { year: 'c. 1470', text: 'Johannes Ockeghem writes a mass that can be sung in whichever mode the singers choose.' },
  { year: 'c. 1780', text: 'Joseph Haydn publishes a game for composing minuets with dice.' },
  { year: '1787', text: 'A dice game attributed to Mozart promises waltzes "without understanding anything about music or composition".' },
  { year: '1952', text: "John Cage's 4′33″: the sounds of the room become the piece." },
  { year: '1957', text: 'Iannis Xenakis composes with probability distributions.' },
  { year: '1964', text: "Terry Riley's In C leaves the form to the performers." },
  { year: '2024', text: 'BruQner hands the choices to entangled photons.' },
];
---
<Base title="The physics" description="How entangled photons conduct music: randomness, entanglement, Bell's test and the experiment we take on stage.">
  <header class="wrap intro">
    <h1>Two photons, one score</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      On stage, a laser makes pairs of entangled photons and two stations, Alice and Bob, measure
      them about twice a second. Each result on its own is pure chance. Compared with each other,
      the results agree more often than anything in classical physics can arrange. That surplus of
      agreement is what the music is made of.
    </p>
    <nav class="toc ui" aria-label="On this page">
      <ol>
        {sections.map((s) => <li><a href={`#${s.id}`}>{s.title}</a></li>)}
      </ol>
    </nav>
  </header>

  <Photo
    src={pumpLaser}
    alt="A violet laser beam leaves the laser module and passes through optics on a dark breadboard."
    credit="weitblickfilm"
    caption="The 405 nm pump laser, the only light in the experiment you can see."
    bleed
    loading="eager"
    ratio="21 / 9"
  />

  <section id="chance" class="wrap chapter">
    <h2>Chance</h2>
    <div class="body">
      <p class="big">A dice roll only looks random. A quantum measurement is random.</p>
      <p>
        In classical physics, everything has a cause. Where a roulette ball stops seems random only
        because the motion is too complicated to follow: the randomness is ours, a lack of
        knowledge. Whether a single photon passes through a polarising beam splitter or is reflected
        has no cause at all. The odds are 50:50 and nothing in the universe decides the outcome in
        advance.
      </p>
      <p>
        Composers have used chance for centuries. All of them used the first kind. <em>The Sound of
        Entanglement</em> brings in the second kind, and something more: correlations between two
        random results that no classical process can produce.
      </p>
      <ol class="lineage">
        {lineage.map((l) => (
          <li><span class="year num">{l.year}</span><span>{l.text}</span></li>
        ))}
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="entanglement" class="wrap chapter">
    <h2>Entanglement</h2>
    <div class="body">
      <p class="big">Two photons with one shared fate, and no script written in advance.</p>
      <p>
        Think of a pair of gloves packed into two boxes and sent to Alice and Bob. When Alice opens
        hers and finds a left glove, she knows at once that Bob has the right one. Nothing mysterious
        happened: the outcome was decided when the gloves were packed.
      </p>
      <p>
        Entangled photons are not like that. The polarisation of each photon does not exist before it
        is measured, so each outcome is genuinely random. Yet when the two results are compared, they
        are correlated. The pair has to be described as one thing: the whole is more than the sum of
        its parts. Erwin Schrödinger called this
        "not <em>one</em> but rather <em>the</em> characteristic trait of quantum mechanics".
      </p>
      <p>
        In 1935 Einstein, Podolsky and Rosen argued that this could not be the whole story. The
        photons must carry hidden instructions, like the gloves, which we simply cannot see. For
        thirty years this looked like a question for philosophers.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="bell" class="wrap chapter">
    <h2>Bell's test</h2>
    <div class="body">
      <p class="big">In 1964 John Bell turned the argument into a number you can measure.</p>
      <ol class="steps">
        <li>Alice chooses at random between two measurement settings, <span class="alice">a<sub>1</sub></span> or <span class="alice">a<sub>2</sub></span>. Bob chooses between <span class="bob">b<sub>1</sub></span> and <span class="bob">b<sub>2</sub></span>.</li>
        <li>Each gets a result, +1 or −1.</li>
        <li>Over many pairs, they work out how strongly their results agree for each of the four combinations of settings. Each of these correlations, E, lies between −1 (always opposite) and +1 (always the same).</li>
        <li>Three of the four are added and the fourth is subtracted. The result is the Bell value, <span class="formula">S = |E<sub>11</sub> + E<sub>12</sub> + E<sub>21</sub> − E<sub>22</sub>|</span>.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        If the photons carried hidden instructions, S could never be larger than 2. Entangled photons
        can reach 2√2, about 2.83. Our experiment reaches about 2.4 to 2.6 on stage. Experiments that
        settled the question beyond doubt were awarded the Nobel Prize in Physics in 2022.
      </p>
      <details>
        <summary class="ui">Why hidden instructions can't get above 2</summary>
        <div class="details-body">
          <p>
            Suppose every pair carries a fixed answer for every setting: A<sub>1</sub> and A<sub>2</sub>
            for Alice, B<sub>1</sub> and B<sub>2</sub> for Bob, each +1 or −1. Then for every single
            pair, A<sub>1</sub>(B<sub>1</sub> + B<sub>2</sub>) + A<sub>2</sub>(B<sub>1</sub> − B<sub>2</sub>)
            is either +2 or −2. Either B<sub>1</sub> and B<sub>2</sub> are equal, and the second bracket
            is zero, or they differ, and the first one is. The table checks all sixteen cases.
          </p>
          <ClassicalProof />
          <p>
            The average of numbers that are each +2 or −2 cannot be larger than 2. That average is
            exactly the S above. So any conductor that sends instructions in advance, whether a human
            or a classical computer, is stuck at 2.
          </p>
        </div>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary class="ui">Why we measure 2.4 to 2.6, not 2.83</summary>
        <div class="details-body">
          <p>
            2√2 is the value for a perfect source, perfect optics and perfect detectors. Ours is a
            portable setup that is flown to venues and aligned in a few hours. Slight imperfections in
            the entangled state, differences between detectors, occasional coincidences between
            unrelated photons and slow drifts of the polarisation all pull the value down. What
            matters is that it stays clearly above 2.
          </p>
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="try" class="wrap chapter lab-chapter">
    <h2>Run it yourself</h2>
    <div class="body wide">
      <p>
        Here are Alice and Bob, each choosing a setting at random for every pair. Watch the four
        correlations fill in and the Bell value settle. With a handful of pairs it jumps around wildly:
        the piece <a href="/pieces/indeterminate-apparatus/"><em>Indeterminate Apparatus</em></a> uses
        exactly that noise as its tempo. Then switch to a classical source.
      </p>
      <BellLab measured={measured} />
      <p class="measured-list ui dim">
        Measured at our shows:{' '}
        {measured.map((m, i) => (
          <>
            <a href={`/shows/${m.id}/`}>{m.label}</a> <span class="num">{m.value.toFixed(2)}</span>{i < measured.length - 1 ? '; ' : '.'}
          </>
        ))}
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="pairs" class="wrap chapter">
    <h2>Making the pairs</h2>
    <div class="body wide">
      <p class="big narrow">Shine a violet laser through a special crystal, and very rarely one photon splits into two.</p>
      <ConesDiagram />
      <div class="two">
        <p>
          The pump is a 405 nm violet laser. It passes through two thin crystals of beta barium borate,
          set at right angles to each other. In a process called spontaneous parametric
          down-conversion, about one pump photon in a hundred billion splits into two photons with half
          its energy and twice its wavelength, 810 nm: infrared, invisible to the eye.
        </p>
        <p>
          Energy and momentum are conserved, so the two photons leave on opposite sides of a cone around
          the laser beam. The first crystal makes horizontally polarised pairs, the second vertically
          polarised ones, on almost the same cone. For a pair taken from where the cones overlap, there
          is no way to tell which crystal made it, and that is what entangles them:{' '}
          <span class="formula nowrap">(|HH⟩ + |VV⟩)/√2</span>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="measuring" class="wrap chapter">
    <h2>Measuring</h2>
    <div class="body">
      <p class="big">About twice a second, the experiment makes one musical decision.</p>
      <ol class="steps">
        <li>On each side, a small motor turns a half-wave plate to one of two angles, chosen at random. This is the choice of setting. It clicks.</li>
        <li>A polarising beam splitter sends the photon either straight through (+1) or sideways (−1).</li>
        <li>Optical fibres carry it to one of four single-photon detectors: two for Alice, two for Bob.</li>
        <li>A time tagger stamps every detection to within a few trillionths of a second. Two detections less than a nanosecond apart belong to the same pair.</li>
        <li>One pair is picked as the result, <span class="formula">[Alice's setting, Bob's setting, Alice's result, Bob's result]</span>, and sent over the network to the music and the visuals.</li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="wrap measuring-media">
    <div class="m-photo">
      <Photo
        src={setupBeams}
        alt="The breadboard from above, with the red and blue paths of the two photons drawn in, and an inset of the laser module."
        credit="Benjamin Orthner"
        caption="The experiment from above. Red: the photon to Alice. Blue: the photon to Bob."
        sizes="(min-width: 1000px) 60vw, 100vw"
      />
    </div>
    <div class="m-side">
      <figure class="loop">
        <video src="/media/loops/setting-choice-mount.mp4" muted loop playsinline autoplay preload="metadata" aria-label="A red motorised mount turning a half-wave plate, close up." data-loop></video>
        <figcaption class="ui">A half-wave plate turning to a new setting. <span class="credit">Video: The Sound of Entanglement</span></figcaption>
      </figure>
      <AudioPlayer src="/media/audio/wave-plate-clicks.mp3" title="The click of a measurement" note="The motors turning, recorded with a contact microphone. In 8 Rooms these clicks are the metronome." />
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="wrap">
    <Photo
      src={caseTop}
      alt="The complete experiment inside its glass case, lit by an LED strip."
      credit="Benjamin Orthner"
      caption="The whole experiment fits on a 60 × 60 cm breadboard in a glass case, and travels by plane."
      sizes="100vw"
      class="case-photo"
    />
  </div>

  <section id="limits" class="wrap chapter">
    <h2>What it does not show</h2>
    <div class="body">
      <p>
        Our setup is built for the stage, not to settle the question of hidden variables. Alice and Bob
        are about thirty centimetres apart, the settings are chosen by a computer, and not every photon
        is detected. In principle these gaps, called loopholes, would still leave room for a classical
        explanation of our own numbers. They have all been closed in dedicated experiments since 2015.
        We use a phenomenon that is established, and make it audible.
      </p>
      <p>
        Nothing travels between Alice and Bob, and no message can be sent with entangled photons. The
        correlations only appear when the two lists of results are brought together and compared, which
        is what happens, live, in the music.
      </p>
      <p class="more">
        The full description is in our paper, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.08892">"The Sound of
        Entanglement" (arXiv:2509.08892)</a>. For how each piece uses the results, see <a href="/pieces/">the pieces</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<script>
  // Respect reduced motion for the looping video.
  if (window.matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)').matches) {
    document.querySelectorAll<HTMLVideoElement>('video[data-loop]').forEach((v) => {
      v.removeAttribute('autoplay');
      v.pause();
      v.controls = true;
    });
  }
</script>

<style>
  .intro { padding-block: clamp(4rem, 12vw, 9rem) clamp(2.5rem, 6vw, 4rem); }
  .intro h1 { max-width: 14ch; }
  .lede { font-size: var(--step-1); line-height: 1.5; margin-top: 2rem; max-width: 40ch; }
  .toc ol {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    margin: 2.5rem 0 0;
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.5rem 1.75rem;
    counter-reset: toc;
  }
  .toc li { counter-increment: toc; }
  .toc a { text-decoration: none; color: var(--ash); font-size: 0.9375rem; }
  .toc a::before { content: counter(toc) ' '; color: var(--smoke); }
  .toc a:hover { color: var(--paper); }

  .chapter {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(12, minmax(0, 1fr));
    column-gap: var(--gutter);
    padding-block: clamp(4rem, 9vw, 7rem) 0;
  }
  .chapter h2 {
    grid-column: 1 / span 3;
    font-size: var(--step-2);
    position: sticky;
    top: calc(var(--header-h) + 1.5rem);
    align-self: start;
    color: var(--ash);
  }
  .body { grid-column: 4 / span 7; }
  .body.wide { grid-column: 4 / -1; }
  .big {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-weight: 300;
    font-size: var(--step-3);
    line-height: 1.2;
    margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
    max-width: 24ch;
  }
  .narrow { max-width: 24ch; }

  .lineage { list-style: none; padding: 0; margin: 2.5rem 0 0; border-top: 1px solid var(--graphite); }
  .lineage li {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 6.5rem 1fr;
    gap: 1rem;
    padding: 0.7rem 0;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--graphite);
    font-size: 1rem;
  }
  .lineage li:last-child span:last-child { color: var(--paper); }
  .lineage li span:last-child { color: var(--ash); }
  .year { font-family: var(--font-display); color: var(--paper); }

  .steps { padding-left: 1.3rem; margin: 0 0 1.5rem; }
  .steps li { margin-bottom: 0.9rem; padding-left: 0.4rem; max-width: var(--measure); }
  .steps li::marker { font-family: var(--font-display); color: var(--ash); }
  .formula { font-style: normal; white-space: normal; }
  .nowrap { white-space: nowrap; }
  sub { font-size: 0.7em; }

  details {
    border-top: 1px solid var(--graphite);
    margin-top: 1rem;
  }
  details:last-of-type { border-bottom: 1px solid var(--graphite); }
  summary {
    cursor: pointer;
    padding: 1rem 0;
    font-size: 1rem;
    list-style: none;
    display: flex;
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: 1rem;
  }
  summary::-webkit-details-marker { display: none; }
  summary::after { content: '+'; color: var(--ash); font-size: 1.25rem; line-height: 1; }
  details[open] summary::after { content: '−'; }
  .details-body { padding-bottom: 1.5rem; }
  .details-body :global(.proof) { margin: 1.5rem 0; }

  .lab-chapter .body > p:first-child { max-width: var(--measure); margin-bottom: 2rem; }
  .measured-list { margin-top: 1rem; font-size: 0.875rem; line-height: 1.7; max-width: none; }
  .measured-list a { color: var(--ash); }

  .two {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    gap: var(--gutter);
    margin-top: 2.5rem;
  }
  .two p { font-size: 1.0625rem; }

  .measuring-media {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(12, minmax(0, 1fr));
    column-gap: var(--gutter);
    row-gap: 2rem;
    margin-top: 3rem;
    align-items: start;
  }
  .m-photo { grid-column: 1 / span 7; }
  .m-side { grid-column: 8 / -1; }
  .loop { margin: 0 0 1.5rem; }
  .loop video { width: 100%; aspect-ratio: 16 / 9; object-fit: cover; background: var(--soot); }
  .loop figcaption { margin-top: 0.6rem; color: var(--ash); font-size: 0.8125rem; }
  .loop .credit { color: var(--smoke); margin-left: 0.5rem; }
  .measuring-media + .wrap { margin-top: clamp(3rem, 7vw, 5rem); }
  .wrap :global(.case-photo) { max-width: 1100px; margin-left: auto; }

  .more { color: var(--ash); }

  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .chapter h2 { grid-column: 1 / -1; position: static; margin-bottom: 1.5rem; }
    .body, .body.wide { grid-column: 1 / -1; }
    .two { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 0; }
    .m-photo, .m-side { grid-column: 1 / -1; }
    .lineage li { grid-template-columns: 5rem 1fr; }
  }
</style>
